fact_preservation.judge:
You are given (1) a numbered list of atomic facts about the design-pattern role, responsibilities and relationships of a Java class, and (2) a generated summary of that class. Each fact states exactly one thing. Judge only from the summary text; you do not have the source code.

Summarised class: {class_name}
References in the summary such as "this class", "the class" or "it" refer to {class_name}.

For each fact, assign exactly one label:
- PRESERVED: the summary states the fact explicitly or through an unambiguous paraphrase that refers to the same class or method and the same role or relationship.
- OMITTED: the summary does not state the fact, states it only partly or only hedged ("may", "possibly"), or only implies it by naming the pattern without linking it to the specific classes.
- INCORRECT: the summary states something that contradicts the fact (e.g. assigns the role to a different class, names a different pattern, reverses a relationship). If the summary both states and contradicts a fact, use INCORRECT.

Matching rules:
- Class names match on the simple name; ignore package qualifiers, letter case and formatting such as backticks.
- Method names match on the name; ignore parameter lists.
- Do not use your own knowledge of the pattern to fill gaps.

Return only a JSON object, without code fences or any other text, with exactly one entry per fact in the order given:
{{
  "facts": [{{"id": <int>, "evidence": "<verbatim summary quote that supports or contradicts the fact; null if OMITTED>", "justification": "<one sentence>", "label": "PRESERVED|OMITTED|INCORRECT"}}]
}}

Facts:
{facts}

Summary:
{summary}
